WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.203
A mouse passes its hearing test after loud noise.

2
00:00:07.043 --> 00:00:08.423
Can its cochlea still be damaged?

3
00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:12.887
A threshold is the quietest sound that evokes a response.

4
00:00:13.807 --> 00:00:15.387
That is what a hearing test measures.

5
00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:20.110
Inner hair cells pass sound to nerve fibres at ribbon synapses.

6
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.250
The ribbon holds a ready supply of vesicles.

7
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:29.183
Kujawa and Liberman exposed male mice of one lab strain, sixteen weeks old.

8
00:00:30.003 --> 00:00:31.623
The loud noise lasted two hours.

9
00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:37.693
Thresholds rose after the noise, then returned to their pre-exposure values by two

10
00:00:37.693 --> 00:00:40.253
weeks. The hair cells remained intact.

11
00:00:41.283 --> 00:00:44.863
Eight weeks later, what would you predict for the number of ribbon synapses?

12
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:54.003
About half were gone at the 32 kHz place.

13
00:00:54.003 --> 00:00:56.723
Nerve terminals had been lost early.

14
00:00:57.683 --> 00:01:02.643
Losing these synapses while the hair cells survive is called cochlear synaptopathy.

15
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:07.443
Why would losing half the synapses leave the threshold where it was?

16
00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:11.433
Nerve fibres at one place differ in sensitivity.

17
00:01:12.493 --> 00:01:15.913
Some respond to very quiet sounds, others need louder sound.

18
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:21.917
A threshold asks when the response to a quiet sound first stands out from background

19
00:01:21.917 --> 00:01:22.917
firing.

20
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.170
At the quietest levels, only the most sensitive fibres do that.

21
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.460
Here is a small model of sixteen fibres.

22
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.320
It is illustrative and passive, not fitted to mouse data.

23
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:39.313
Remove half the fibres at random, or remove the most sensitive half.

24
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.193
Which moves the threshold?

25
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:51.467
Losing a random half moved the threshold by about 2 dB.

26
00:01:52.247 --> 00:01:54.827
A hearing test moves in 5 dB steps.

27
00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:59.550
Losing the most sensitive half moved it by about 8 dB.

28
00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.370
That is more than one 5 dB step.

29
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:08.053
In this model, the sound-driven response to a loud sound halved in every case.

30
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.633
So here is the one idea.

31
00:02:11.713 --> 00:02:15.153
Hearing runs through a chain: hair cell, ribbon synapse, nerve fibre.

32
00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:19.357
A threshold depends mostly on the most sensitive links.

33
00:02:20.237 --> 00:02:21.377
It does not count the links.

34
00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:26.907
In these mice, a cochlea lost many synapses and still passed a hearing test.

35
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.080
And a repair has to put back what is missing: a molecule in a surviving cell, or the

36
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.080
cell itself.

37
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.550
In people, temporal bones show nerve fibre loss after death.

38
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.570
No one can count synapses in a living ear.

39
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:47.010
Living ears give only indirect signals, and the evidence from them is mixed.

40
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.250
A normal threshold is good news about sensitivity.

41
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.830
It is not a census of synapses.
